1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,586
A pull request is GitHub's proposal to merge
one branch into another, and the merge

2
00:00:09,626 --> 00:00:14,651
box is where GitHub says whether it may
merge. A required check is a result that a

3
00:00:14,691 --> 00:00:19,654
rule says must be reported as passing
first. Every pull request that touches only

4
00:00:19,694 --> 00:00:21,692
documentation is stuck like this.

5
00:00:22,032 --> 00:00:26,128
There's no red check to click on,
no log to read, no failed step.

6
00:00:26,168 --> 00:00:30,518
Someone suggests re-running the
workflow. There is nothing to re-run.

7
00:00:30,558 --> 00:00:34,017
Someone else starts the
workflow by hand on the branch.

8
00:00:34,057 --> 00:00:36,881
It goes green, and the
merge box still waits.

9
00:00:37,221 --> 00:00:41,868
Why would a green run, started by hand,
not count? Say your guess out loud.

10
00:00:46,708 --> 00:00:51,480
Both attempts were reasonable and both were
wrong, for reasons that are written in

11
00:00:51,520 --> 00:00:56,351
the documentation. A workflow that was
filtered out never reports the name the rule

12
00:00:56,391 --> 00:01:00,987
is waiting for. And a run started by hand
isn't evaluated for the pull request.

13
00:01:01,027 --> 00:01:05,741
This video is about the design that makes
the situation impossible, and about the

14
00:01:05,781 --> 00:01:09,966
instruments you use when a run does
exist. That design is one small job.

15
00:01:10,006 --> 00:01:10,729
Watch for it.

16
00:01:12,569 --> 00:01:16,292
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

17
00:01:16,332 --> 00:01:21,104
A workflow is a file that tells GitHub
Actions which jobs to run when an event

18
00:01:21,144 --> 00:01:26,162
happens, and a run is one execution of it.
This is the last teaching video of Part

19
00:01:26,202 --> 00:01:31,221
6. It gathers what you need at the moment
a pull request is stuck or a run is red.

20
00:01:31,561 --> 00:01:35,002
Four topics, in the order of
sections 20B.13 to 20B.15.

21
00:01:35,042 --> 00:01:40,256
Required checks that stay pending: five
situations, what the check reports in each,

22
00:01:40,296 --> 00:01:45,193
and one design that survives all of them.
The debugging instruments: reading a

23
00:01:45,233 --> 00:01:50,004
failure, re-running and what a re-run
really is, debug logging, and managing

24
00:01:50,044 --> 00:01:54,624
workflows from the terminal. Linting:
what can be caught before the push.

25
00:01:54,664 --> 00:01:56,840
And the limits of a local emulator.

26
00:01:57,180 --> 00:02:02,886
The demonstration uses Lab 28.1. Its six
workflow files are in workflows/broken/.

27
00:02:02,926 --> 00:02:08,419
They have faults on purpose. Never copy
them into a repository you care about.

28
00:02:08,459 --> 00:02:14,094
The lab has you add one at a time to your
practice repository, observe, fix, and

29
00:02:14,134 --> 00:02:14,591
remove.

30
00:02:16,431 --> 00:02:18,009
Here's what you'll walk away with.

31
00:02:18,349 --> 00:02:19,762
After this video you can:

32
00:02:20,102 --> 00:02:24,305
give the causes of a required check that
waits forever and the design that avoids

33
00:02:24,345 --> 00:02:24,567
each;

34
00:02:24,907 --> 00:02:29,442
diagnose six broken workflows, each
with a different documented root cause;

35
00:02:29,782 --> 00:02:34,529
use the instruments: failed-step logs,
rerun with debug logging, watch, cache

36
00:02:34,569 --> 00:02:35,026
listing;

37
00:02:35,366 --> 00:02:39,957
say exactly what gh run rerun re-runs and
why that is dangerous for a deployment;

38
00:02:40,297 --> 00:02:44,640
say what a linter finds before a push and
what a local emulator cannot tell you.

39
00:02:46,480 --> 00:02:51,711
Required checks that stay pending. The
ruleset side is GitHub, from Chapter 18, and

40
00:02:51,751 --> 00:02:56,601
a ruleset is a named list of rules for
branches. The reporting side is GitHub

41
00:02:56,641 --> 00:03:01,618
Actions. A check is required by name, and
here's what reports that name in five

42
00:03:01,658 --> 00:03:02,317
situations.

43
00:03:02,657 --> 00:03:08,485
The workflow never started, because of a
path filter, a branch filter, or [skip ci]

44
00:03:08,525 --> 00:03:13,221
in the commit message. The check
reports nothing. It stays pending.

45
00:03:13,261 --> 00:03:14,706
The merge is blocked.

46
00:03:15,046 --> 00:03:18,286
The workflow started and the
job was skipped by its if.

47
00:03:18,326 --> 00:03:21,923
The check reports success, because
skipped counts as passing.

48
00:03:21,963 --> 00:03:23,175
The merge is allowed.

49
00:03:23,515 --> 00:03:28,840
Quick quiz, before the third row. A job
is skipped because a job it needs failed.

50
00:03:28,880 --> 00:03:32,814
Does that block the merge? A,
yes, because something failed.

51
00:03:32,854 --> 00:03:36,523
B, no, because a skipped job
doesn't block. Your answer?

52
00:03:41,363 --> 00:03:44,403
B. A job was skipped because
a job it needs failed.

53
00:03:44,443 --> 00:03:49,355
It's skipped, so it may not block. The
merge is allowed, and the textbook adds one

54
00:03:49,395 --> 00:03:54,247
word: wrongly. If that surprised you, good.
It surprises almost everyone, and the

55
00:03:54,287 --> 00:03:56,723
design that follows exists because of it.

56
00:03:57,063 --> 00:04:00,426
The check ran on a workflow_dispatch
run of the head branch.

57
00:04:00,466 --> 00:04:03,942
It's not evaluated for the pull
request. The merge is blocked.

58
00:04:04,282 --> 00:04:07,814
A merge queue is used and the
workflow lacks on: merge_group.

59
00:04:07,854 --> 00:04:12,734
A merge queue merges pull requests one group
at a time, and runs the required checks

60
00:04:12,774 --> 00:04:15,896
on each group first. Nothing
is reported in the queue.

61
00:04:15,936 --> 00:04:17,887
The merge is blocked in the queue.

62
00:04:18,227 --> 00:04:22,365
Three of those five block a merge
that has nothing wrong with it.

63
00:04:22,405 --> 00:04:27,700
Two let a merge through without a real
result. And a path filter has its own edges,

64
00:04:27,740 --> 00:04:32,906
which you heard in video 143: a push with
more than 1,000 commits always runs the

65
00:04:32,946 --> 00:04:37,984
workflow, and with more than 3,000 changed
files a match beyond the first 3,000

66
00:04:38,024 --> 00:04:38,691
isn't seen.

67
00:04:39,031 --> 00:04:44,175
The robust design. The textbook is careful
about its status: the Phase 0 report

68
00:04:44,215 --> 00:04:48,571
labels it an inference from these
rules. Don't filter the workflow.

69
00:04:48,611 --> 00:04:52,377
Start it always. Decide inside
which jobs to run, with if.

70
00:04:52,417 --> 00:04:57,626
And require one aggregate job that always
runs and fails when something it needs

71
00:04:57,666 --> 00:04:59,201
failed or was cancelled.

72
00:04:59,541 --> 00:05:04,358
Read it against the table. The workflow
has no filter, so the name is always

73
00:05:04,398 --> 00:05:09,278
reported: that removes row one. The job's
own if is !cancelled(), so it's not

74
00:05:09,318 --> 00:05:12,857
skipped when a needed job
fails: that removes row three.

75
00:05:12,897 --> 00:05:17,393
And its one step fails the job when
any needed result is a failure or a

76
00:05:17,433 --> 00:05:21,547
cancellation. Require all-checks
in the ruleset and nothing else.

77
00:05:21,587 --> 00:05:26,020
The matrix and the job list can then
change without touching the rule.

78
00:05:26,060 --> 00:05:28,768
There's the one small
job from the opening.

79
00:05:29,108 --> 00:05:31,567
The instruments. Read the failure first.

80
00:05:31,907 --> 00:05:37,979
All 🟢 SAFE: they change nothing. --log-failed
prints the log of the failed steps

81
00:05:38,019 --> 00:05:43,862
only. gh run watch follows a run until
it ends and, with --exit-status, exits

82
00:05:43,902 --> 00:05:48,217
non-zero when it fails, so it
can be chained in a script.

83
00:05:48,257 --> 00:05:52,648
gh pr checks exits with status
8 while checks are pending.

84
00:05:52,688 --> 00:05:56,927
The flags are from the help
output of GitHub CLI 2.88.1.

85
00:05:57,267 --> 00:06:01,626
Re-run. The textbook starts with a
correction: a re-run isn't a new run.

86
00:06:01,666 --> 00:06:06,576
It uses, in the documentation's words,
"the same GITHUB_SHA and GITHUB_REF of the

87
00:06:06,616 --> 00:06:11,220
original event", and the privileges of
the actor who triggered the original.

88
00:06:11,260 --> 00:06:14,153
It's possible for 30 days,
and at most 50 times.

89
00:06:14,493 --> 00:06:19,826
gh run rerun is 🟡 CAUTION. What it changes:
a new attempt of the same run, at the

90
00:06:19,866 --> 00:06:23,209
original commit and ref.
New checks on that commit.

91
00:06:23,249 --> 00:06:27,123
And for a deploy workflow, a
new deployment of that commit.

92
00:06:27,163 --> 00:06:32,629
That last part is the danger. Re-running
last week's deploy run deploys last week's

93
00:06:32,669 --> 00:06:38,135
commit over today's. The preview: gh run
view with the JSON field headSha, compared

94
00:06:38,175 --> 00:06:43,442
with main. The textbook's rule: use a
re-run to retry a flaky step of the latest

95
00:06:43,482 --> 00:06:46,161
run, and a fresh run for everything else.

96
00:06:46,501 --> 00:06:51,944
Debug logging. Two switches, each a
repository secret or variable set to true.

97
00:06:51,984 --> 00:06:57,568
The secret wins if both exist. ACTIONS_STEP_DEBUG
adds debug lines to step logs.

98
00:06:57,608 --> 00:07:03,261
ACTIONS_RUNNER_DEBUG adds runner and worker
diagnostic logs to the downloaded log

99
00:07:03,301 --> 00:07:08,955
archive. For one run, gh run rerun --debug,
or the checkbox on the re-run dialog,

100
00:07:08,995 --> 00:07:14,579
does the same without leaving the switch
on. Anyone who may run the workflow may

101
00:07:14,619 --> 00:07:15,281
enable it.

102
00:07:15,621 --> 00:07:21,186
Skipped jobs. Since the twenty-ninth of
January 2026 the log of a skipped job shows

103
00:07:21,226 --> 00:07:24,630
the original if expression
and its expanded values.

104
00:07:24,970 --> 00:07:26,373
Run and manage workflows.

105
00:07:26,713 --> 00:07:32,107
The listing and viewing commands are 🟢 SAFE.
gh workflow run is 🟡 CAUTION: it starts

106
00:07:32,147 --> 00:07:37,477
a run, and on a deploy workflow, a deployment.
The preview is to read the file with

107
00:07:37,517 --> 00:07:42,782
gh workflow view --yaml. It needs on:
workflow_dispatch in the file on the default

108
00:07:42,822 --> 00:07:48,217
branch. And, as in the hook, a run started
this way doesn't satisfy a required check

109
00:07:48,257 --> 00:07:49,381
of a pull request.

110
00:07:49,721 --> 00:07:54,753
One warning. Dumping the whole github
context into a log is a documented debugging

111
00:07:54,793 --> 00:07:58,960
aid, and that context contains the
token. Print the fields you need.

112
00:07:59,300 --> 00:08:03,564
Linting. Linting means checking a
file for mistakes without running it.

113
00:08:03,604 --> 00:08:08,171
Catch what can be caught before the
push. actionlint checks workflow syntax,

114
00:08:08,211 --> 00:08:12,961
expression types and embedded shell. The
GitHub Actions language service in the

115
00:08:13,001 --> 00:08:17,689
editor does part of that while you type.
Neither is installed for this course.

116
00:08:17,729 --> 00:08:22,600
The course's own check is only that each
file parses as YAML and has on and jobs,

117
00:08:22,640 --> 00:08:25,510
which finds indentation
errors and nothing else.

118
00:08:25,850 --> 00:08:30,887
Then the sentence that sets the expectation:
all six broken workflows of Lab 28.1

119
00:08:30,927 --> 00:08:34,523
pass that check, and a linter
would pass most of them too.

120
00:08:34,563 --> 00:08:39,350
A linter proves that a file is well
formed. It can't know that the history is

121
00:08:39,390 --> 00:08:41,669
shallow or that a secret is withheld.

122
00:08:42,009 --> 00:08:46,119
A local emulator. act runs workflows
locally in Docker containers.

123
00:08:46,159 --> 00:08:51,401
Its own documentation lists what it doesn't
implement: concurrency, job permissions,

124
00:08:51,441 --> 00:08:56,180
environment, OIDC, timeout-minutes,
continue-on-error, step summaries, and a

125
00:08:56,220 --> 00:09:01,084
complete github context. The textbook's
verdict: useful for the shell logic of

126
00:09:01,124 --> 00:09:06,177
steps, and useless for exactly the topics
of this chapter: gates, tokens, groups,

127
00:09:06,217 --> 00:09:11,333
runner images. Treat a green local run as
evidence about your scripts, never about

128
00:09:11,373 --> 00:09:12,340
your deployment.

129
00:09:14,180 --> 00:09:15,153
A picture helps.

130
00:09:15,493 --> 00:09:20,123
A required check is a name, and the rule
waits for somebody to say that name with a

131
00:09:20,163 --> 00:09:20,517
result.

132
00:09:20,857 --> 00:09:25,621
Picture a roll call. The rule reads a name
from its list and waits for an answer.

133
00:09:25,661 --> 00:09:30,247
If the workflow that owns the name was
never started, nobody is in the room to

134
00:09:30,287 --> 00:09:34,754
answer, and the rule keeps waiting. It
doesn't conclude "absent means fine".

135
00:09:34,794 --> 00:09:39,558
If the job was in the room and was told
by its if to sit this one out, it answers

136
00:09:39,598 --> 00:09:41,159
"present", and that counts.

137
00:09:41,499 --> 00:09:45,770
Where the picture breaks: at a roll
call, anyone can see the empty chair.

138
00:09:45,810 --> 00:09:50,611
In a merge box, a check that will never
report and a check that will report in two

139
00:09:50,651 --> 00:09:55,571
minutes look alike. You tell them apart by
asking whether a run exists at all, which

140
00:09:55,611 --> 00:09:57,756
is step 1 of the investigation order.

141
00:09:58,096 --> 00:10:02,329
The aggregate job is the one person who
is always in the room and answers for

142
00:10:02,369 --> 00:10:03,771
everyone else, truthfully.

143
00:10:04,111 --> 00:10:09,622
And a model for the instruments: each
one is tied to a step of the order.

144
00:10:09,662 --> 00:10:14,944
gh run view with JSON fields is steps
1 and 2. --log-failed is step 9.

145
00:10:14,984 --> 00:10:18,974
gh run download is step 10.
gh cache list is step 11.

146
00:10:19,014 --> 00:10:25,284
gh run list for the workflow is step 12.
If you reach for an instrument, know which

147
00:10:25,324 --> 00:10:26,425
step you're on.

148
00:10:28,265 --> 00:10:33,978
Try it now, on paper. Thirty seconds. Draw
a ruleset that requires the name X, and

149
00:10:34,018 --> 00:10:39,660
two workflows that could say it: one without
a filter, and one with a path filter

150
00:10:39,700 --> 00:10:43,379
for src. A pull request
changes only docs/runbook.md.

151
00:10:43,419 --> 00:10:46,676
Under each workflow, write
what the rule hears.

152
00:10:49,016 --> 00:10:53,546
From the first: success or failure,
so the rule gets its answer.

153
00:10:53,586 --> 00:10:57,831
From the second: nothing. No
run exists, and the rule waits.

154
00:10:59,671 --> 00:11:00,431
Into the lab.

155
00:11:00,771 --> 00:11:05,591
The lab's instruction comes first: for each
file, write your hypothesis and the line

156
00:11:05,631 --> 00:11:07,905
you suspect before you collect evidence.

157
00:11:08,245 --> 00:11:14,246
Workflow 1: "the version step fails with
exit status 128 on every push to main."

158
00:11:14,586 --> 00:11:18,465
git clone is 🟢 SAFE. This case
is the one from the last video.

159
00:11:18,505 --> 00:11:21,563
Say the cause out loud
before the output appears.

160
00:11:26,403 --> 00:11:31,748
One commit, no tags, and "No names found"
with status 128 in the clone that is like

161
00:11:31,788 --> 00:11:35,512
the job's. In your own repository,
the proper description.

162
00:11:35,852 --> 00:11:41,071
Workflow 2: "a pull request that changes
only docs cannot be merged; the merge box

163
00:11:41,111 --> 00:11:44,662
waits for the required check
unit-tests; no run exists."

164
00:11:45,002 --> 00:11:49,850
The workflow has a path filter for src.
For which of the two branches would it

165
00:11:49,890 --> 00:11:51,605
start? Make your prediction.

166
00:11:56,445 --> 00:11:59,796
The documentation branch
changed one file under docs.

167
00:11:59,836 --> 00:12:03,763
The feature branch changed a file
under src and the lock file.

168
00:12:04,103 --> 00:12:07,317
The workflow starts for the
second and not for the first.

169
00:12:07,357 --> 00:12:11,655
Its job is a required check. This is
the hook, proven with a three-dot diff.

170
00:12:11,995 --> 00:12:16,782
Workflow 5: "runs on feature branches end
as cancelled although nobody cancelled

171
00:12:16,822 --> 00:12:17,144
them."

172
00:12:17,484 --> 00:12:21,301
Three refs receive pushes. You
saw this snippet in video 150.

173
00:12:21,341 --> 00:12:26,295
A concurrency group must tell them apart,
and the report says it happens when a

174
00:12:26,335 --> 00:12:30,215
colleague pushes to a different
branch at about the same time.

175
00:12:30,255 --> 00:12:32,617
Open the file and read its group name.

176
00:12:32,957 --> 00:12:37,601
Workflow 6: "a pull request added a dependency
and updated the lock file; the tests

177
00:12:37,641 --> 00:12:42,229
fail in the job because the new package
cannot be imported; the install step shows

178
00:12:42,269 --> 00:12:42,906
as skipped."

179
00:12:43,246 --> 00:12:48,234
git rev-parse with a ref, a colon and a path
prints the ID of the blob at that path,

180
00:12:48,274 --> 00:12:50,927
and a blob is the stored
content of one file.

181
00:12:51,267 --> 00:12:56,227
If the two IDs differ, what must be true
of a correct cache key on the two refs?

182
00:12:56,267 --> 00:12:57,227
Say it out loud.

183
00:13:02,067 --> 00:13:07,317
Two different blob IDs, d420909 on main
and dfb5599 on the branch, and four added

184
00:13:07,357 --> 00:13:12,802
lines. The lock file differs, so a key built
from the lock file must differ too, and

185
00:13:12,842 --> 00:13:17,765
the branch must get a miss. The report
says the install step was skipped, so

186
00:13:17,805 --> 00:13:23,185
something was restored. Read the file's key
and its restore keys, and its condition

187
00:13:23,225 --> 00:13:24,165
for installing.

188
00:13:24,505 --> 00:13:25,484
The tempting repair.

189
00:13:25,824 --> 00:13:30,275
--always. The command now prints
57c8425 and the step is green.

190
00:13:30,315 --> 00:13:35,978
The comment in the transcript names it:
a repair that hides the fault instead of

191
00:13:36,018 --> 00:13:41,396
fixing it. Section 20B.17 has a list of
these: continue-on-error, "or true",

192
00:13:41,436 --> 00:13:46,671
--always, broader restore keys. Each
hides a cause from the next engineer.

193
00:13:47,011 --> 00:13:51,551
The real repair, in Git terms:
the full history and the tags.

194
00:13:51,591 --> 00:13:54,479
git fetch --unshallow --tags is 🟢 SAFE.

195
00:13:54,819 --> 00:13:59,764
Workflows 3 and 4 you diagnose by reading.
The reports: for 3, the step that

196
00:13:59,804 --> 00:14:05,209
comments on the pull request fails for every
pull request, including those from the

197
00:14:05,249 --> 00:14:09,014
repository's own branches, and
the API refuses with a 403.

198
00:14:09,054 --> 00:14:14,524
For 4, green for every maintainer and red
for the first pull request from an outside

199
00:14:14,564 --> 00:14:20,034
contributor's fork, with the workflow's own
message that a secret is empty, although

200
00:14:20,074 --> 00:14:21,214
the secret exists.

201
00:14:21,554 --> 00:14:26,635
For each, read the file against the event
that started the run, and ask step 3 and

202
00:14:26,675 --> 00:14:27,884
step 7 of the order.

203
00:14:28,224 --> 00:14:32,620
Part B of Lab 28.1, on your practice
repository, in your normal shell.

204
00:14:32,660 --> 00:14:37,881
The interface changes. The lab text and the
linked documentation are the reference.

205
00:14:37,921 --> 00:14:42,888
No GitHub output was captured by the
authors. These six reports are constructed

206
00:14:42,928 --> 00:14:47,768
descriptions written from the documented
behavior. They aren't captured logs.

207
00:14:48,108 --> 00:14:53,716
Add one broken file at a time. For each:
observe, fix it yourself, observe again,

208
00:14:53,756 --> 00:14:57,760
remove it. The lab asks for at
least workflows 1, 3 and 5.

209
00:14:58,100 --> 00:15:03,193
For workflow 1, after the push, list the
runs and print the failed step's log with

210
00:15:03,233 --> 00:15:08,389
the instrument for step 9. For workflow 3,
open the failed step and find the status

211
00:15:08,429 --> 00:15:13,648
code. Then read the permissions of the file.
For workflow 5, push to two branches in

212
00:15:13,688 --> 00:15:18,217
quick succession and look at the run
list for the conclusion of each run.

213
00:15:18,257 --> 00:15:23,413
Each time, before you fix anything, say
which step of the order gave you the cause.

214
00:15:25,253 --> 00:15:26,901
Five mistakes to watch for.

215
00:15:27,241 --> 00:15:31,959
Requiring a check that a filterable workflow
owns. Root cause: a workflow that never

216
00:15:31,999 --> 00:15:35,470
started reports nothing, and the
required check stays pending.

217
00:15:35,810 --> 00:15:40,330
Trying to satisfy a pull request's
required check with gh workflow run.

218
00:15:40,370 --> 00:15:45,725
Root cause: a check from a workflow_dispatch
run of the head branch is not evaluated

219
00:15:45,765 --> 00:15:47,074
for the pull request.

220
00:15:47,414 --> 00:15:53,082
An aggregate job without !cancelled(). Root
cause: a job that needs a failed job is

221
00:15:53,122 --> 00:15:56,177
skipped, and a skipped
job counts as passing.

222
00:15:56,517 --> 00:16:01,627
Re-running an old deploy run. Root cause:
a re-run uses the same commit and ref as

223
00:16:01,667 --> 00:16:04,893
the original event, so it
deploys old code over new.

224
00:16:05,233 --> 00:16:10,385
Treating a green local emulator run as
proof. Root cause: the emulator does not

225
00:16:10,425 --> 00:16:15,709
implement concurrency, job permissions,
environments or OIDC, and has no complete

226
00:16:15,749 --> 00:16:16,694
github context.

227
00:16:18,534 --> 00:16:23,418
Now, out of the lab. A platform team with
a busy repository adopts a merge queue to

228
00:16:23,458 --> 00:16:28,342
stop pull requests from going out of date.
On the first morning the queue fills and

229
00:16:28,382 --> 00:16:31,901
nothing leaves it. Every entry
waits for the required check.

230
00:16:32,241 --> 00:16:36,631
Go to the table. Row five: a merge queue
is used and the workflow lacks on:

231
00:16:36,671 --> 00:16:41,592
merge_group. Nothing is reported in the
queue. The workflow listens for pull_request

232
00:16:41,632 --> 00:16:45,432
and for push, and neither of those
is the event of a merge group.

233
00:16:45,772 --> 00:16:50,591
The fix is one more event in the trigger
of the workflow that owns the required

234
00:16:50,631 --> 00:16:55,573
name. The lesson is the same as for the
path filter: for every way a merge can be

235
00:16:55,613 --> 00:16:59,203
attempted, ask whether anything
will say the required name.

236
00:16:59,243 --> 00:17:04,308
With one aggregate job as the only required
check, that question has to be answered

237
00:17:04,348 --> 00:17:05,846
for exactly one workflow.

238
00:17:07,686 --> 00:17:12,732
Your turn. Do Lab 28.1,
"Six broken workflows", in

239
00:17:12,772 --> 00:17:16,699
lab-manual/m28-runners-debugging-ci.md.

240
00:17:17,039 --> 00:17:21,703
For each of the six, before any evidence:
the hypothesis, the suspected line, and

241
00:17:21,743 --> 00:17:26,465
the layer. After the evidence: the root
cause from the documentation, the smallest

242
00:17:26,505 --> 00:17:30,995
correct fix, and what would have prevented
it. Each of the six has a different

243
00:17:31,035 --> 00:17:35,234
documented root cause. If two of your
answers are the same, one is wrong.

244
00:17:35,574 --> 00:17:41,678
The challenge is Exercise 28.7,
"Every pull request is waiting", in

245
00:17:41,718 --> 00:17:45,163
exercises/m26-m31-actions-security.md.

246
00:17:49,003 --> 00:17:53,478
Answer out loud. A strong answer starts
from what "required" means: a name, and

247
00:17:53,518 --> 00:17:58,278
something that must report it. It gives
three distinct causes for nothing reporting,

248
00:17:58,318 --> 00:18:02,964
each with the documented mechanism, and
then one design, with the reason each part

249
00:18:03,004 --> 00:18:04,278
of the design is there.

250
00:18:04,618 --> 00:18:08,657
It says how you would confirm which
cause applies before changing anything.

251
00:18:08,697 --> 00:18:13,225
The follow-up asks how you predict, before
pushing, whether a path-filtered workflow

252
00:18:13,265 --> 00:18:17,740
will start, and where that prediction breaks
down. You know the command, and you've

253
00:18:17,780 --> 00:18:19,589
heard the two edges of the filter.

254
00:18:21,429 --> 00:18:22,356
Let's land this.

255
00:18:22,696 --> 00:18:24,356
You should now be able to say:

256
00:18:24,696 --> 00:18:29,285
A check is required by name; a workflow
that was filtered out never reports that

257
00:18:29,325 --> 00:18:33,278
name, and a manually started run
does not count for the pull request.

258
00:18:33,618 --> 00:18:38,580
A job skipped by its if counts as passing,
and so does a job skipped because a

259
00:18:38,620 --> 00:18:39,735
needed job failed.

260
00:18:40,075 --> 00:18:45,628
The design that avoids all of it: no filter
on the workflow, decisions inside jobs,

261
00:18:45,668 --> 00:18:50,143
and one aggregate job with !cancelled()
as the only required check.

262
00:18:50,483 --> 00:18:55,265
A re-run is a new attempt at the original
commit and ref; on a deploy workflow it is

263
00:18:55,305 --> 00:18:56,872
a rollback nobody announced.

264
00:18:57,212 --> 00:19:01,849
A linter proves a file is well formed;
a local emulator is evidence about your

265
00:19:01,889 --> 00:19:05,628
scripts and never about gates,
tokens, groups or runner images.

266
00:19:07,468 --> 00:19:14,988
Read sections 20B.13 to 20B.18 of Chapter
20B and do the Practice section 20B.20.

267
00:19:15,028 --> 00:19:20,495
Do Exercise 28.3, "A required
check that cannot be red", in

268
00:19:20,535 --> 00:19:27,775
exercises/m26-m31-actions-security.md.
Read guides/github-actions-guide.md and

269
00:19:27,815 --> 00:19:31,696
cheatsheets/github-actions-cheat-sheet.md.

270
00:19:32,036 --> 00:19:37,554
That was the last teaching video of Part 6.
A stuck merge box is no longer a mystery

271
00:19:37,594 --> 00:19:42,451
to you: you ask whether a run exists,
and who would say the required name.

272
00:19:42,491 --> 00:19:45,892
Do Lab 28.1 with a written
hypothesis for each file.

273
00:19:45,932 --> 00:19:51,252
Next time: the gate briefing for Actions.
Until then, look at the state first and

274
00:19:51,292 --> 00:19:53,700
type second. See you in the next one.
